{"schema_version":1,"id":"018f0000-0000-7000-8000-00000000c6a7","stream":{"kind":"session","id":"01a0785b-9847-7fa3-b40c-313061570fc0"},"sequence":430,"recorded_at":1780531400000855,"record_type":"event","durability":"durable","causation_id":"5f283545-6d77-4246-bbdf-5a980fc43a74","payload_type":"tool.result","payload_schema_version":1,"payload":{"kind":"tool_result","command_id":"5f283545-6d77-4246-bbdf-5a980fc43a74","run_stream":{"kind":"run","id":"5f283545-6d77-4246-bbdf-5a980fc43a74"},"call_id":"call_01a0785ca9c57322b4a6a15524cd60f7","text":"<read-skill-result name=\"go-testing-tools\" status=\"ok\">\n<metadata>\npath: /Users/iv/.agents/skills/go-testing-tools/SKILL.md\nlocator: file:///Users/iv/.agents/skills/go-testing-tools/SKILL.md\nsource: user\nbytes_returned: 15216\nbytes_total: 15216\ntruncated: false\n</metadata>\n\n<skill-body id=\"go-testing-tools\">\n---\nname: go-testing-tools\ndescription: &gt;\n  Go testing toolkit with tuitestkit library and a closed-loop development\n  cycle for Go applications. Covers test helpers, reducer tests,\n  snapshot/golden testing, mock executor patterns, and view assertions.\ntriggers:\n  - go test\n  - bubbletea test\n  - tuitestkit\n  - go test helpers\n  - reducer test\n  - snapshot test go\n  - golden file go\n  - тесты го\n  - го тест хелперы\n  - тесты баблти\n  - тест утилиты го\n  - замкнутый цикл тестирования\n---\n\n# Go Testing Tools\n\nTesting toolkit for Go TUI applications built with bubbletea. Provides a Go library (`tuitestkit`), reference documentation, and templates that enable a closed-loop development cycle: write code, write tests, run tests, validate, fix, repeat -- all without human intervention.\n\nCore philosophy: **Elm architecture makes TUI testable.** The reducer is a pure function -- given the same state and action, it always returns the same result. No I/O, no randomness, no terminal. Test everything as function calls.\n\n---\n\n## Prerequisites\n\n- **Go 1.21+**\n- **bubbletea** (`github.com/charmbracelet/bubbletea`)\n- **lipgloss** (`github.com/charmbracelet/lipgloss`) -- used by most bubbletea apps for styling\n\nInstall the library in your project:\n\n```bash\ngo get github.com/relux-works/skill-go-testing-tools/tuitestkit\n```\n\n---\n\n## Quick Start\n\nMinimal test showing the core workflow: build a message, send it to a model, assert on the view.\n\n```go\npackage myscreen_test\n\nimport (\n    &quot;testing&quot;\n\n    &quot;github.com/relux-works/skill-go-testing-tools/tuitestkit&quot;\n)\n\nfunc TestBasicNavigation(t *testing.T) {\n    m := NewMyModel(testData)\n    m = tuitestkit.Send(m, tuitestkit.WindowSize(80, 24))\n\n    // Press down twice\n    m = tuitestkit.Send(m, tuitestkit.Key(&quot;down&quot;), tuitestkit.Key(&quot;down&quot;))\n\n    // Assert the view contains expected content\n    tuitestkit.ViewContains(t, m, &quot;third item&quot;)\n    tuitestkit.ViewNotContains(t, m, &quot;ERROR&quot;)\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n## Testing Pyramid\n\nFive levels, from fastest/most numerous to slowest/fewest:\n\n| Level | What | Speed | Investment |\n|-------|------|-------|------------|\n| 1. Pure Reducer | `func(State, Action) State` | Nanoseconds | ~60% |\n| 2. Component | `Update` + `View` | Microseconds | ~20% |\n| 3. Integration | Multi-component with mocks | Milliseconds | ~10% |\n| 4. Snapshot | Golden file comparison | Milliseconds | ~5% |\n| 5. Behavioral | Full user workflows | Milliseconds | ~5% |\n\nWhen time is limited: **Level 1 &gt; Level 2 &gt; Level 3**. Pure reducer tests catch the most bugs per line of test code.\n\nFull details: `references/testing-pyramid.md`\n\n---\n\n## Library API Reference\n\nAll functions are in the `tuitestkit` package.\n\n### Message Builders (`messages.go`)\n\nBuild `tea.Msg` values for driving model updates.\n\n```go\n// Key builds a tea.KeyMsg from a human-readable string.\n// Supports: &quot;enter&quot;, &quot;tab&quot;, &quot;esc&quot;, &quot;space&quot;, &quot;backspace&quot;, &quot;up&quot;, &quot;down&quot;,\n// &quot;left&quot;, &quot;right&quot;, &quot;home&quot;, &quot;end&quot;, &quot;pgup&quot;, &quot;pgdown&quot;, &quot;delete&quot;, &quot;insert&quot;,\n// &quot;f1&quot;-&quot;f20&quot;, &quot;ctrl+c&quot;, &quot;ctrl+a&quot;...&quot;ctrl+z&quot;, &quot;alt+h&quot;, &quot;alt+enter&quot;,\n// single runes: &quot;a&quot;, &quot;/&quot;, &quot;1&quot;, etc.\nfunc Key(k string) tea.KeyMsg\n\n// Keys builds a slice of tea.Msg from multiple key strings.\nfunc Keys(keys ...string) []tea.Msg\n\n// WindowSize builds a tea.WindowSizeMsg with the given dimensions.\nfunc WindowSize(w, h int) tea.WindowSizeMsg\n\n// MouseClick builds a tea.MouseMsg for a left-button click at (x, y).\nfunc MouseClick(x, y int) tea.MouseMsg\n\n// MouseClickRight builds a tea.MouseMsg for a right-button click at (x, y).\nfunc MouseClickRight(x, y int) tea.MouseMsg\n\n// MouseScroll builds a tea.MouseMsg for a scroll event.\n// Direction constants: ScrollUp, ScrollDown, ScrollLeft, ScrollRight.\nfunc MouseScroll(dir ScrollDir) tea.MouseMsg\n\n// MouseRelease builds a tea.MouseMsg for a button release at (x, y).\nfunc MouseRelease(x, y int) tea.MouseMsg\n```\n\n### Model Harness (`harness.go`)\n\nSend messages to bubbletea models and handle commands.\n\n```go\n// Send sends messages to a model sequentially, returns the final model.\n// Preserves concrete type via generics -- no type assertion needed.\n// Panics if Update returns a different type (broken implementation).\n// Init() is NOT called.\nfunc Send[M tea.Model](model M, msgs ...tea.Msg) M\n\n// SendAndCollect sends messages, collects all non-nil Cmds returned by Update.\n// Returns final model + collected commands.\nfunc SendAndCollect[M tea.Model](model M, msgs ...tea.Msg) (M, []tea.Cmd)\n\n// ExecCmds executes tea.Cmd functions synchronously, collects resulting messages.\n// Recursively handles tea.BatchMsg. Nil cmds/messages are skipped.\nfunc ExecCmds(cmds ...tea.Cmd) []tea.Msg\n```\n\n**Common pattern -- command pipeline:**\n\n```go\nm, cmds := tuitestkit.SendAndCollect(m, tuitestkit.Key(&quot;r&quot;))\nmsgs := tuitestkit.ExecCmds(cmds...)\nm = tuitestkit.Send(m, msgs...)\n```\n\n### Reducer Harness (`reducer.go`)\n\nTable-driven testing for pure reducer functions.\n\n```go\n// ReducerTest defines a single test case for a pure reducer.\n// S = state type, A = action type.\ntype ReducerTest[S any, A any] struct {\n    Name    string\n    Initial S\n    Action  A\n    Assert  func(t *testing.T, got S)\n}\n\n// Step defines one step in a multi-action reducer sequence.\n// Assert is optional -- if nil, applied without per-step validation.\ntype Step[S any, A any] struct {\n    Name   string\n    Action A\n    Assert func(t *testing.T, got S)\n}\n\n// ReducerSequence defines a multi-step test scenario.\n// Actions applied sequentially from Initial. Final runs on end state.\ntype ReducerSequence[S, A any] struct {\n    Name    string\n    Initial S\n    Steps   []Step[S, A]\n    Final   func(t *testing.T, got S)\n}\n\n// RunReducerTests executes table-driven reducer tests as subtests.\nfunc RunReducerTests[S, A any](t *testing.T, reduce func(S, A) S, tests []ReducerTest[S, A])\n\n// RunReducerSequences executes multi-step sequence tests.\n// Per-step Assert runs after each step (if non-nil). Final runs on end state.\nfunc RunReducerSequences[S, A any](t *testing.T, reduce func(S, A) S, sequences []ReducerSequence[S, A])\n```\n\n**Invariant checking:**\n\n```go\n// Invariant defines a property that must hold for any state.\ntype Invariant[S any] struct {\n    Name  string\n    Check func(s S) error\n}\n\n// InvariantChecker holds invariants and validates state against all of them.\ntype InvariantChecker[S any] struct { /* ... */ }\n\n// NewInvariantChecker creates a checker with the given invariants.\nfunc NewInvariantChecker[S any](invariants ...Invariant[S]) *InvariantChecker[S]\n\n// Check validates state against all invariants. Returns combined error or nil.\nfunc (ic *InvariantChecker[S]) Check(s S) error\n\n// WrapWithInvariants wraps a reducer with invariant checking.\n// After every reduce call, all invariants are validated.\n// t.Fatalf on violation.\nfunc WrapWithInvariants[S, A any](t *testing.T, reduce func(S, A) S, checker *InvariantChecker[S]) func(S, A) S\n```\n\n**Example:**\n\n```go\nchecker := tuitestkit.NewInvariantChecker(\n    tuitestkit.Invariant[AppState]{\n        Name: &quot;cursor in bounds&quot;,\n        Check: func(s AppState) error {\n            if s.Cursor &lt; 0 || s.Cursor &gt;= len(s.Items) {\n                return fmt.Errorf(&quot;cursor %d out of [0, %d)&quot;, s.Cursor, len(s.Items))\n            }\n            return nil\n        },\n    },\n)\nsafeReduce := tuitestkit.WrapWithInvariants(t, Reduce, checker)\ntuitestkit.RunReducerTests(t, safeReduce, tests)\n```\n\n### Mock Building Blocks (`mock.go`)\n\nComposable primitives for building project-specific mocks.\n\n```go\n// MockCall represents a single recorded method invocation.\ntype MockCall struct {\n    Method string\n    Args   []any\n}\n\n// MockCallRecorder provides thread-safe recording of method calls.\n// Embed into project-specific mock structs.\ntype MockCallRecorder struct { /* ... */ }\n\nfunc (r *MockCallRecorder) Record(method string, args ...any)\nfunc (r *MockCallRecorder) CallCount(method string) int\nfunc (r *MockCallRecorder) Calls() []MockCall\nfunc (r *MockCallRecorder) CallsFor(method string) []MockCall\nfunc (r *MockCallRecorder) Reset()\n\n// MockResponseMap provides thread-safe canned response storage.\ntype MockResponseMap struct { /* ... */ }\n\nfunc NewMockResponseMap() *MockResponseMap\nfunc (m *MockResponseMap) Set(key string, data []byte, err error)\nfunc (m *MockResponseMap) Get(key string) ([]byte, error)\nfunc (m *MockResponseMap) SetError(key string, err error)\n\n// Assertion helpers\nfunc AssertCalled(t testing.TB, r *MockCallRecorder, method string)\nfunc AssertNotCalled(t testing.TB, r *MockCallRecorder, method string)\nfunc AssertCalledN(t testing.TB, r *MockCallRecorder, method string, n int)\nfunc AssertCalledWith(t testing.TB, r *MockCallRecorder, method string, args ...any)\n```\n\n**Building a mock (two lines per method):**\n\n```go\ntype TaskBoardMock struct {\n    tuitestkit.MockCallRecorder\n    Responses *tuitestkit.MockResponseMap\n}\n\nfunc NewTaskBoardMock() *TaskBoardMock {\n    return &amp;TaskBoardMock{Responses: tuitestkit.NewMockResponseMap()}\n}\n\nfunc (m *TaskBoardMock) TreeJSON() ([]byte, error) {\n    m.Record(&quot;TreeJSON&quot;)\n    return m.Responses.Get(&quot;TreeJSON&quot;)\n}\n\nfunc (m *TaskBoardMock) Execute(cmd string, args ...string) ([]byte, error) {\n    m.Record(&quot;Execute&quot;, cmd, args)\n    return m.Responses.Get(&quot;Execute:&quot; + cmd)\n}\n```\n\n### View Assertions (`view.go`)\n\nAssert on rendered output. All functions strip ANSI escape codes before comparison.\n\n```go\n// StripANSI removes all ANSI escape sequences from a string.\nfunc StripANSI(s string) string\n\n// --- Model-based (call model.View() internally) ---\n\nfunc ViewContains(t testing.TB, model tea.Model, text string)\nfunc ViewNotContains(t testing.TB, model tea.Model, text string)\nfunc ViewLines(model tea.Model) []string\nfunc ViewLineContains(t testing.TB, model tea.Model, lineIdx int, text string)\nfunc ViewLineEquals(t testing.TB, model tea.Model, lineIdx int, text string)\nfunc ViewMatchesRegex(t testing.TB, model tea.Model, pattern string)\n\n// --- String-based (when you already have the view string) ---\n\nfunc ContainsStr(t testing.TB, view string, text string)\nfunc NotContainsStr(t testing.TB, view string, text string)\nfunc LinesFromStr(view string) []string\nfunc MatchesRegexStr(t testing.TB, view string, pattern string)\n```\n\n### Snapshot Testing (`snapshot.go`)\n\nGolden file comparison for visual regression testing.\n\n```go\n// UpdateSnapshots controls whether snapshots are written (true) or compared (false).\n// Set via UPDATE_SNAPSHOTS=1 environment variable.\nvar UpdateSnapshots bool\n\n// SnapshotView captures model.View(), strips ANSI, compares against golden file.\nfunc SnapshotView(t *testing.T, model tea.Model, name string)\n\n// SnapshotViewRaw captures model.View() with raw ANSI codes intact.\nfunc SnapshotViewRaw(t *testing.T, model tea.Model, name string)\n\n// SnapshotStr compares a pre-rendered string (ANSI-stripped) against golden file.\nfunc SnapshotStr(t *testing.T, view string, name string)\n\n// SnapshotStrRaw compares a pre-rendered string (raw ANSI) against golden file.\nfunc SnapshotStrRaw(t *testing.T, view string, name string)\n```\n\nGolden files stored at `testdata/snapshots/&lt;name&gt;.golden` relative to the test file.\n\n```bash\n# Create or update golden files\nUPDATE_SNAPSHOTS=1 go test ./...\n\n# Update for a single package\nUPDATE_SNAPSHOTS=1 go test ./internal/ui/screens/board/ -run TestSnapshot\n```\n\nMismatches produce a line-by-line diff with `-`/`+` markers.\n\n---\n\n## Closed-Loop Workflow\n\nThe agent development cycle for writing tests:\n\n```\n1. UNDERSTAND: Read screen/component code\n2. IDENTIFY:   What behaviors need tests?\n3. EXTRACT:    If exec.Command present, extract to interface (use mock template)\n4. WRITE:      Tests using tuitestkit helpers\n5. RUN:        go test ./... -v\n6. VALIDATE:   All pass? Check coverage with go test -cover\n7. FIX:        If failures, read error, fix code or test, goto 5\n8. SNAPSHOT:   Optionally capture view golden files\n9. DONE:       Tests green, coverage acceptable\n```\n\n### Step Details\n\n**UNDERSTAND:** Read the component source. Identify:\n- What state does it manage?\n- What messages does it handle in Update?\n- Does it have a pure reducer (`func(State, Action) State`)?\n- Does it shell out to external commands (exec.Command)?\n- What does View() render?\n\n**IDENTIFY:** Map behaviors to test levels:\n- Pure state logic -&gt; Level 1 (reducer tests)\n- Key bindings, view rendering -&gt; Level 2 (component tests)\n- Data loading, command execution -&gt; Level 3 (integration tests with mocks)\n- Visual appearance -&gt; Level 4 (snapshot tests)\n\n**EXTRACT:** If the component uses `exec.Command` or any I/O directly:\n1. Define an interface with only the methods this component needs\n2. Move `exec.Command` calls to a production implementation\n3. Build a mock using `MockCallRecorder` + `MockResponseMap`\n4. See `assets/templates/executor_interface.go.tmpl` and `assets/templates/mock_executor.go.tmpl`\n\n**WRITE:** Use the appropriate tuitestkit helpers per level:\n- Level 1: `RunReducerTests`, `RunReducerSequences`, `WrapWithInvariants`\n- Level 2: `Send`, `ViewContains`, `ViewLines`, `Key`, `MouseClick`\n- Level 3: `SendAndCollect`, `ExecCmds`, `AssertCalled`, `AssertCalledWith`\n- Level 4: `SnapshotView`, `SnapshotStr`\n\n**RUN:** `go test ./... -v` -- all tests must run in under 1 second (no I/O, no network).\n\n**VALIDATE:** `go test ./... -cover` -- target 80%+ for code under test.\n\n**FIX:** Read error messages carefully. Common issues:\n- Type assertion panic -&gt; `Update` returns wrong type (broken implementation)\n- View assertion failure -&gt; check ANSI stripping, check exact text\n- Snapshot mismatch -&gt; run with `UPDATE_SNAPSHOTS=1` if the change is intentional\n- Mock assertion failure -&gt; check response key matches (`&quot;Execute:list&quot;` not `&quot;Execute&quot;`)\n\n---\n\n## Templates\n\nScaffolding templates in `assets/templates/`:\n\n| Template | Purpose |\n|----------|---------|\n| `reducer_test.go.tmpl` | Table-driven reducer tests with invariants |\n| `component_test.go.tmpl` | Component model tests (keyboard, mouse, commands, view) |\n| `snapshot_test.go.tmpl` | Snapshot golden file tests |\n| `executor_interface.go.tmpl` | Executor interface + production stub |\n| `mock_executor.go.tmpl` | Mock executor using MockCallRecorder + MockResponseMap |\n| `PROJECT_STRUCTURE.md` | Recommended directory layout for test files |\n\nCopy templates into your project, replace `TODO` markers with your actual types and import paths.\n\n---\n\n## References\n\nIn-depth documentation in `references/`:\n\n| Document | Content |\n|----------|---------|\n| `elm-architecture.md` | Why Elm makes TUI testable, state/action/reducer/view cycle, nested reducers, dependency injection, the executor pattern |\n| `mock-patterns.md` | Interface extraction, when to mock vs test pure functions, stubs vs mocks vs fakes, callback capture, full mock lifecycle example |\n| `testing-pyramid.md` | All 5 test levels with code examples, decision table for which level to use, coverage strategy |\n\n---\n\n## Setup\n\nRun the setup script to create symlinks:\n\n```bash\n./scripts/setup.sh\n```\n\nThis links the skill to `~/.agents/skills/go-testing-tools`, `~/.claude/skills/go-testing-tools`, and `~/.codex/skills/go-testing-tools`.\n\nTo remove:\n\n```bash\n./scripts/deinit.sh\n```\n\n</skill-body>\n</read-skill-result>","correlation_facts":{"tool_name":"read_skill","outcome":"success"}}}
{"schema_version":1,"id":"018f0000-0000-7000-8000-00000000c6a9","stream":{"kind":"session","id":"01a0785b-9847-7fa3-b40c-313061570fc0"},"sequence":431,"recorded_at":1780531400000857,"record_type":"event","durability":"durable","causation_id":"5f283545-6d77-4246-bbdf-5a980fc43a74","payload_type":"tool.result","payload_schema_version":1,"payload":{"kind":"tool_result","command_id":"5f283545-6d77-4246-bbdf-5a980fc43a74","run_stream":{"kind":"run","id":"5f283545-6d77-4246-bbdf-5a980fc43a74"},"call_id":"call_01a0785caa3e7791ab23aee7f7972ad2","text":"Read text file `internal/terminalbackend/terminalbackend.go`.\n1|// Package terminalbackend implements the TerminalBackend registry: canonical\n2|// backend identities, implementation/protocol version tuples, external\n3|// executable trust admission, and duplicate or drift refusal.\n4|//\n5|// Normative scope is relux-works/agent-session-manager-spec@v0.5.0 §4.B\n6|// (registry, identity, discovery, trust), §4.1 (backend interface versions),\n7|// §6.5 (Configuration 3.0.0 TerminalBackend extension), and §7.A (Provider\n8|// Protocol 3.0.0 Terminal Instance binding). Manifest/Probe schemas,\n9|// capability evidence, and generation-bound admission are defined in\n10|// manifest.go; lifecycle operations and legacy v0.4.3 translation belong to\n11|// the sibling story tasks and are deliberately not defined here.\n12|package terminalbackend\n13|\n14|import (\n15|\t\"crypto/sha256\"\n16|\t\"encoding/hex\"\n17|\t\"errors\"\n18|\t\"fmt\"\n19|\t\"os\"\n20|\t\"path/filepath\"\n21|\t\"regexp\"\n22|\t\"sort\"\n23|\t\"strings\"\n24|\t\"sync\"\n25|\t\"unicode/utf8\"\n26|\n27|\t\"github.com/relux-works/agent-session-manager/internal/scalar\"\n28|)\n29|\n30|// Wire error codes are the exact Section 15.3 codes the registry may report.\n31|// They are a subset of the pinned catalog error vocabulary; no other code is\n32|// emitted by this package.\n33|const (\n34|\t// CodeNotFound reports an unknown or malformed backend identity. A failed\n35|\t// read is never absence: callers must not treat this as \"no backend\".\n36|\tCodeNotFound = \"terminal_backend_not_found\"\n37|\t// CodeAmbiguous reports a duplicate or ambiguous backend ID. Re-registering\n38|\t// an identical record is still refused: identity registration is not\n39|\t// idempotent.\n40|\tCodeAmbiguous = \"terminal_backend_ambiguous\"\n41|\t// CodeUntrusted reports a disabled, unresolvable, or digest-mismatched\n42|\t// external executable. Trust is established before any probe.\n43|\tCodeUntrusted = \"terminal_backend_untrusted\"\n44|\t// CodeDrift reports a re-registration whose version tuple, kind,\n45|\t// platforms, or executable digest differs from the admitted record.\n46|\tCodeDrift = \"terminal_backend_implementation_drift\"\n47|\t// CodeRestoreMismatch reports an attempt to substitute the configured\n48|\t// default for the exact prior binding on restore or resume.\n49|\tCodeRestoreMismatch = \"terminal_backend_restore_mismatch\"\n50|\t// CodeStaleGeneration reports a generation that is out of bound or does\n51|\t// not match the validated binding.\n52|\tCodeStaleGeneration = \"terminal_backend_stale_generation\"\n53|)\n54|\n55|// Canonical built-in backend identities. The ax. namespace is reserved (§4.B);\n56|// no third-party registration may use it.\n57|const (\n58|\tBuiltinTmux   = \"ax.tmux\"\n59|\tBuiltinConpty = \"ax.conpty\"\n60|)\n61|\n62|// reservedNamespace is the only namespace this implementation may mint.\n63|const reservedNamespace = \"ax.\"\n64|\n65|// maxIDBytes is the Section 4.B bound: 1-128 ASCII bytes.\n66|const maxIDBytes = 128\n67|\n68|// maxGenerationRunes is the Section 4.B/7.A bound: string[1..256] counts\n69|// UTF-8 characters (SPEC.md:321), not bytes; terminal_backend_id is the\n70|// only member of this package still bounded in ASCII bytes.\n71|const maxGenerationRunes = 256\n72|\n73|// maxProtocolVersions is the Section 4.B bound: sorted unique semver[1..32].\n74|const maxProtocolVersions = 32\n75|\n76|var (\n77|\tidPattern = regexp.MustCompile(`^[a-z][a-z0-9]*(?:[.-][a-z0-9]+)*$`)\n78|\t// semverPattern implements Semantic Versioning 2.0.0 in full, matching the\n79|\t// grammar the canonical and config packages enforce for the same members.\n80|\tsemverPattern = regexp.MustCompile(`^(0|[1-9][0-9]*)\\.(0|[1-9][0-9]*)\\.(0|[1-9][0-9]*)(?:-(?:0|[1-9][0-9]*|[0-9]*[A-Za-z-][0-9A-Za-z-]*)(?:\\.(?:0|[1-9][0-9]*|[0-9]*[A-Za-z-][0-9A-Za-z-]*))*)?(?:\\+[0-9A-Za-z-]+(?:\\.[0-9A-Za-z-]+)*)?$`)\n81|)\n82|\n83|// Error is a registry refusal. Code is always one of the Code* constants;\n84|// BackendID names the identity at issue when there is one. Detail is a static\n85|// clause: it never echoes paths, digests, generations, or other local data.\n86|type Error struct {\n87|\tCode      string\n88|\tBackendID string\n89|\tDetail    string\n90|}\n91|\n92|func (err *Error) Error() string {\n93|\tif err.BackendID == \"\" {\n94|\t\treturn \"terminal backend refused: \" + err.Code + \" at \" + err.Detail\n95|\t}\n96|\treturn \"terminal backend refused: \" + err.Code + \" for \" + err.BackendID + \" at \" + err.Detail\n97|}\n98|\n99|// errorCode reports whether err carries the given wire code.\n100|func errorCode(err error, code string) bool {\n101|\tvar refusal *Error\n102|\tif !errors.As(err, &refusal) {\n103|\t\treturn false\n104|\t}\n105|\treturn refusal.Code == code\n106|}\n107|\n108|// IsNotFound reports CodeNotFound refusals.\n109|func IsNotFound(err error) bool { return errorCode(err, CodeNotFound) }\n110|\n111|// IsAmbiguous reports CodeAmbiguous refusals.\n112|func IsAmbiguous(err error) bool { return errorCode(err, CodeAmbiguous) }\n113|\n114|// IsUntrusted reports CodeUntrusted refusals.\n115|func IsUntrusted(err error) bool { return errorCode(err, CodeUntrusted) }\n116|\n117|// IsDrift reports CodeDrift refusals.\n118|func IsDrift(err error) bool { return errorCode(err, CodeDrift) }\n119|\n120|// IsRestoreMismatch reports CodeRestoreMismatch refusals.\n121|func IsRestoreMismatch(err error) bool { return errorCode(err, CodeRestoreMismatch) }\n122|\n123|// IsStaleGeneration reports CodeStaleGeneration refusals.\n124|func IsStaleGeneration(err error) bool { return errorCode(err, CodeStaleGeneration) }\n125|\n126|// ParseID validates a canonical terminal backend identity: 1-128 ASCII bytes\n127|// matching [a-z][a-z0-9]*(?:[.-][a-z0-9]+)*. It admits the reserved ax.\n128|// namespace only for the two canonical built-ins; every other ax.-prefixed ID\n129|// is refused so a third party cannot mint a trusted-looking identity.\n130|func ParseID(value string) (string, error) {\n131|\tif !utf8.ValidString(value) || len(value) < 1 || len(value) > maxIDBytes {\n132|\t\treturn \"\", &Error{Code: CodeNotFound, Detail: \"terminal_backend_id bound\"}\n133|\t}\n134|\tif !idPattern.MatchString(value) {\n135|\t\treturn \"\", &Error{Code: CodeNotFound, BackendID: value, Detail: \"terminal_backend_id grammar\"}\n136|\t}\n137|\tif strings.HasPrefix(value, reservedNamespace) && value != BuiltinTmux && value != BuiltinConpty {\n138|\t\treturn \"\", &Error{Code: CodeNotFound, BackendID: value, Detail: \"terminal_backend_id reserved namespace\"}\n139|\t}\n140|\treturn value, nil\n141|}\n142|\n143|// mustParseID is the internal admission used where the caller already holds a\n144|// validated record. It duplicates the ParseID rule so a Registry can never\n145|// hold an identity its own grammar refuses.\n146|func mustParseID(value string) error {\n147|\t_, err := ParseID(value)\n148|\treturn err\n149|}\n150|\n151|// Kind is the Section 4.B implementation_kind vocabulary.\n152|type Kind string\n153|\n154|// Implementation kinds. Built-ins run inside the ax binary; externals arrive\n155|// as adapter executables over the Section 4.B argv/framing wire.\n156|const (\n157|\tKindBuiltinGo         Kind = \"builtin_go\"\n158|\tKindLocalProgram      Kind = \"local_program\"\n159|\tKindTrustedExecutable Kind = \"trusted_executable\"\n160|\tKindNativeRuntime     Kind = \"native_runtime\"\n161|)\n162|\n163|// parseKind admits exactly the closed Section 4.B vocabulary.\n164|func parseKind(value string) (Kind, error) {\n165|\tswitch Kind(value) {\n166|\tcase KindBuiltinGo, KindLocalProgram, KindTrustedExecutable, KindNativeRuntime:\n167|\t\treturn Kind(value), nil\n168|\tdefault:\n169|\t\treturn \"\", &Error{Code: CodeNotFound, Detail: \"implementation_kind vocabulary\"}\n170|\t}\n171|}\n172|\n173|// needsDigest reports whether the kind carries an executable digest. Section\n174|// 4.B: digest for local_program or trusted_executable; null otherwise.\n175|func needsDigest(kind Kind) bool {\n176|\treturn kind == KindLocalProgram || kind == KindTrustedExecutable\n177|}\n178|\n179|// Registration is one admitted backend identity with its exact version tuple,\n180|// platform set, and trust binding. It is the registry half of the Section 4.B\n181|// Manifest identity: the Manifest/Probe objects themselves belong to the\n182|// sibling manifest task.\n183|type Registration struct {\n184|\t// ID is the canonical terminal_backend_id.\n185|\tID string\n186|\t// Kind is the closed implementation_kind.\n187|\tKind Kind\n188|\t// ImplementationVersion is the exact admitted semver.\n189|\tImplementationVersion string\n190|\t// ProtocolVersions are the sorted unique semver members in Terminal\n191|\t// Backend Protocol major 1.\n192|\tProtocolVersions []string\n193|\t// Platforms is the sorted unique non-empty platform subset.\n194|\tPlatforms []scalar.Platform\n195|\t// ExecutableDigest is the sha256: digest for external kinds and empty\n196|\t// (null) for built-in kinds.\n197|\tExecutableDigest string\n198|}\n199|\n200|// validate enforces every closed Section 4.B/6.5 rule on one record.\n201|func (record Registration) validate() error {\n202|\tif err := mustParseID(record.ID); err != nil {\n203|\t\treturn err\n204|\t}\n205|\tif _, err := parseKind(string(record.Kind)); err != nil {\n206|\t\treturn err\n207|\t}\n208|\tif !semverPattern.MatchString(record.ImplementationVersion) {\n209|\t\treturn &Error{Code: CodeDrift, BackendID: record.ID, Detail: \"implementation_version semver\"}\n210|\t}\n211|\tif err := validateProtocolVersions(record.ID, record.ProtocolVersions); err != nil {\n212|\t\treturn err\n213|\t}\n214|\tif err := validatePlatforms(record.Platforms); err != nil {\n215|\t\treturn &Error{Code: CodeNotFound, BackendID: record.ID, Detail: err.Error()}\n216|\t}\n217|\tif needsDigest(record.Kind) {\n218|\t\tif _, err := scalar.ParseDigest(record.ExecutableDigest); err != nil {\n219|\t\t\treturn &Error{Code: CodeUntrusted, BackendID: record.ID, Detail: \"executable_digest\"}\n220|\t\t}\n221|\t} else if record.ExecutableDigest != \"\" {\n222|\t\treturn &Error{Code: CodeDrift, BackendID: record.ID, Detail: \"executable_digest must be null\"}\n223|\t}\n224|\treturn nil\n225|}\n226|\n227|// validateProtocolVersions enforces sorted unique semver[1..32], each in\n228|// Terminal Backend Protocol major 1.\n229|func validateProtocolVersions(backendID string, versions []string) error {\n230|\tif len(versions) < 1 || len(versions) > maxProtocolVersions {\n231|\t\treturn &Error{Code: CodeDrift, BackendID: backendID, Detail: \"protocol_versions bound\"}\n232|\t}\n233|\tfor index, version := range versions {\n234|\t\tif !semverPattern.MatchString(version) || semverMajor(version) != 1 {\n235|\t\t\treturn &Error{Code: CodeDrift, BackendID: backendID, Detail: \"protocol_versions major 1\"}\n236|\t\t}\n237|\t\tif index > 0 && versions[index-1] >= version {\n238|\t\t\treturn &Error{Code: CodeDrift, BackendID: backendID, Detail: \"protocol_versions sorted unique\"}\n239|\t\t}\n240|\t}\n241|\treturn nil\n242|}\n243|\n244|// semverMajor returns the major number of a validated semver string.\n245|func semverMajor(version string) int {\n246|\tmajor := 0\n247|\tfor i := 0; i < len(version); i++ {\n248|\t\tif version[i] == '.' {\n249|\t\t\tbreak\n250|\t\t}\n251|\t\tmajor = major*10 + int(version[i]-'0')\n252|\t}\n253|\treturn major\n254|}\n255|\n256|// validatePlatforms enforces a sorted unique non-empty subset of the closed\n257|// macos|linux|wsl2|windows vocabulary.\n258|func validatePlatforms(platforms []scalar.Platform) error {\n259|\tif len(platforms) == 0 || len(platforms) > 4 {\n260|\t\treturn errors.New(\"platforms bound\")\n261|\t}\n262|\tprevious := \"\"\n263|\tfor _, platform := range platforms {\n264|\t\tif _, err := scalar.ParsePlatform(platform.String()); err != nil {\n265|\t\t\treturn errors.New(\"platforms vocabulary\")\n266|\t\t}\n267|\t\tif previous >= platform.String() {\n268|\t\t\treturn errors.New(\"platforms sorted unique\")\n269|\t\t}\n270|\t\tprevious = platform.String()\n271|\t}\n272|\treturn nil\n273|}\n274|\n275|// cloneStrings deep-copies a version list so the registry never shares a\n276|// backing array with its caller. Registration carries slices; a struct copy\n277|// duplicates only the header, so every ingress and egress point must clone.\n278|func cloneStrings(values []string) []string {\n279|\tif values == nil {\n280|\t\treturn nil\n281|\t}\n282|\tcloned := make([]string, len(values))\n283|\tcopy(cloned, values)\n284|\treturn cloned\n285|}\n286|\n287|// clonePlatforms deep-copies a platform set for the same reason as\n288|// cloneStrings: without it a caller holding a resolved record keeps a live\n289|// handle on admitted registry state and can convert drift into a duplicate.\n290|func clonePlatforms(values []scalar.Platform) []scalar.Platform {\n291|\tif values == nil {\n292|\t\treturn nil\n293|\t}\n294|\tcloned := make([]scalar.Platform, len(values))\n295|\tcopy(cloned, values)\n296|\treturn cloned\n297|}\n298|\n299|// cloneRecord deep-copies every slice member of a record.\n300|func cloneRecord(record Registration) Registration {\n301|\trecord.ProtocolVersions = cloneStrings(record.ProtocolVersions)\n302|\trecord.Platforms = clonePlatforms(record.Platforms)\n303|\treturn record\n304|}\n305|\n306|// equalRecord reports whether two validated records are member-for-member\n307|// identical. Any difference is drift, never a compatible update.\n308|func equalRecord(left, right Registration) bool {\n309|\tif left.ID != right.ID || left.Kind != right.Kind ||\n310|\t\tleft.ImplementationVersion != right.ImplementationVersion ||\n311|\t\tleft.ExecutableDigest != right.ExecutableDigest {\n312|\t\treturn false\n313|\t}\n314|\tif len(left.ProtocolVersions) != len(right.ProtocolVersions) {\n315|\t\treturn false\n316|\t}\n317|\tfor i := range left.ProtocolVersions {\n318|\t\tif left.ProtocolVersions[i] != right.ProtocolVersions[i] {\n319|\t\t\treturn false\n320|\t\t}\n321|\t}\n322|\tif len(left.Platforms) != len(right.Platforms) {\n323|\t\treturn false\n324|\t}\n325|\tfor i := range left.Platforms {\n326|\t\tif left.Platforms[i] != right.Platforms[i] {\n327|\t\t\treturn false\n328|\t\t}\n329|\t}\n330|\treturn true\n331|}\n332|\n333|// TrustEntry is one Configuration 3.0.0 external_trust entry (§6.5): exactly a\n334|// backend ID, an absolute executable path, an executable digest, and enabled.\n335|type TrustEntry struct {\n336|\tBackendID        string\n337|\tExecutablePath   string\n338|\tExecutableDigest string\n339|\tEnabled          bool\n340|}\n341|\n342|// validateTrust enforces the Section 6.5 trust admission: grammar plus the\n343|// reserved-namespace bar (third parties use a vendor-owned namespace),\n344|// absolute path only (PATH-only discovery fails), and a well-formed digest.\n345|// A disabled entry is not an error here; registration treats it as untrusted.\n346|func (entry TrustEntry) validate(platform scalar.Platform) error {\n347|\tif err := mustParseID(entry.BackendID); err != nil {\n348|\t\treturn err\n349|\t}\n350|\tif strings.HasPrefix(entry.BackendID, reservedNamespace) {\n351|\t\treturn &Error{Code: CodeAmbiguous, BackendID: entry.BackendID, Detail: \"external_trust reserved namespace\"}\n352|\t}\n353|\tif _, err := scalar.ParseAbsolutePath(platform, entry.ExecutablePath); err != nil {\n354|\t\treturn &Error{Code: CodeUntrusted, BackendID: entry.BackendID, Detail: \"external_trust executable_path\"}\n355|\t}\n356|\tif _, err := scalar.ParseDigest(entry.ExecutableDigest); err != nil {\n357|\t\treturn &Error{Code: CodeUntrusted, BackendID: entry.BackendID, Detail: \"external_trust executable_digest\"}\n358|\t}\n359|\treturn nil\n360|}\n361|\n362|// Registry is the process-local TerminalBackend registry. It opens with the\n363|// two canonical built-ins admitted and accepts external adapters only through\n364|// RegisterExternal with a validated trust entry. The zero value is unusable;\n365|// construct with New.\n366|type Registry struct {\n367|\tmutex   sync.RWMutex\n368|\trecords map[string]Registration\n369|}\n370|\n371|// New admits the canonical built-ins ax.tmux (macos, linux, wsl2) and\n372|// ax.conpty (windows) at the given implementation version with the given\n373|// Terminal Backend Protocol versions. Both are builtin_go without an\n374|// executable digest. Unknown members or a non-major-1 protocol list fail\n375|// before either built-in is admitted.\n376|func New(implementationVersion string, protocolVersions []string) (*Registry, error) {\n377|\tif !semverPattern.MatchString(implementationVersion) {\n378|\t\treturn nil, &Error{Code: CodeDrift, Detail: \"implementation_version semver\"}\n379|\t}\n380|\tprotocols := append([]string(nil), protocolVersions...)\n381|\tsort.Strings(protocols)\n382|\tif err := validateProtocolVersions(BuiltinTmux, protocols); err != nil {\n383|\t\treturn nil, err\n384|\t}\n385|\tregistry := &Registry{records: make(map[string]Registration, 4)}\n386|\tbuiltins := []Registration{\n387|\t\t{\n388|\t\t\tID:                    BuiltinTmux,\n389|\t\t\tKind:                  KindBuiltinGo,\n390|\t\t\tImplementationVersion: implementationVersion,\n391|\t\t\t// Each built-in gets its own copy so the two admitted records\n392|\t\t\t// never alias each other. This is defence-in-depth, not a\n393|\t\t\t// bypass fix: callers only ever see egress clones (Resolve\n394|\t\t\t// clones, RegisterExternal clones on ingress, IDs returns\n395|\t\t\t// strings), so no exported path can reach these arrays.\n396|\t\t\tProtocolVersions: cloneStrings(protocols),\n397|\t\t\tPlatforms:        []scalar.Platform{scalar.PlatformLinux, scalar.PlatformMacOS, scalar.PlatformWSL2},\n398|\t\t\t// Platforms literal above is already sorted: linux < macos < wsl2.\n399|\t\t},\n400|\t\t{\n401|\t\t\tID:                    BuiltinConpty,\n402|\t\t\tKind:                  KindBuiltinGo,\n403|\t\t\tImplementationVersion: implementationVersion,\n404|\t\t\tProtocolVersions:      cloneStrings(protocols),\n405|\t\t\tPlatforms:             []scalar.Platform{scalar.PlatformWindows},\n406|\t\t},\n407|\t}\n408|\tfor _, record := range builtins {\n409|\t\tif err := record.validate(); err != nil {\n410|\t\t\treturn nil, err\n411|\t\t}\n412|\t\tregistry.records[record.ID] = record\n413|\t}\n414|\treturn registry, nil\n415|}\n416|\n417|// RegisterExternal admits one external adapter (§4.B discovery before\n418|// activation, §6.5 trust). The trust entry must be enabled and name an\n419|// absolute executable whose digest exactly equals the observed record digest;\n420|// a mismatch fails before any probe. The observed record must use an external\n421|// kind, carry the same ID, and pass full record validation. Registering an\n422|// already-known ID fails closed: an identical record is CodeAmbiguous\n423|// (registration is not idempotent) and any difference is CodeDrift.\n424|func (registry *Registry) RegisterExternal(platform scalar.Platform, entry TrustEntry, observed Registration) error {\n425|\tif registry == nil {\n426|\t\treturn &Error{Code: CodeNotFound, Detail: \"registry unavailable\"}\n427|\t}\n428|\tif err := entry.validate(platform); err != nil {\n429|\t\treturn err\n430|\t}\n431|\tif !entry.Enabled {\n432|\t\treturn &Error{Code: CodeUntrusted, BackendID: entry.BackendID, Detail: \"external_trust disabled\"}\n433|\t}\n434|\tif observed.ID != entry.BackendID {\n435|\t\treturn &Error{Code: CodeAmbiguous, BackendID: entry.BackendID, Detail: \"external_trust identity binding\"}\n436|\t}\n437|\tif observed.Kind != KindLocalProgram && observed.Kind != KindTrustedExecutable {\n438|\t\treturn &Error{Code: CodeUntrusted, BackendID: entry.BackendID, Detail: \"external implementation_kind\"}\n439|\t}\n440|\tif err := observed.validate(); err != nil {\n441|\t\treturn err\n442|\t}\n443|\tif observed.ExecutableDigest != entry.ExecutableDigest {\n444|\t\treturn &Error{Code: CodeUntrusted, BackendID: entry.BackendID, Detail: \"executable substitution\"}\n445|\t}\n446|\tregistry.mutex.Lock()\n447|\tdefer registry.mutex.Unlock()\n448|\texisting, known := registry.records[observed.ID]\n449|\tif !known {\n450|\t\t// Store a copy: the caller keeps its own record, and retaining\n451|\t\t// the slices verbatim would leave the admitted record mutable\n452|\t\t// without passing any gate.\n453|\t\tregistry.records[observed.ID] = cloneRecord(observed)\n454|\t\treturn nil\n455|\t}\n456|\tif equalRecord(existing, observed) {\n457|\t\treturn &Error{Code: CodeAmbiguous, BackendID: observed.ID, Detail: \"duplicate backend_id\"}\n458|\t}\n459|\treturn &Error{Code: CodeDrift, BackendID: observed.ID, Detail: \"implementation drift\"}\n460|}\n461|\n462|// Resolve returns the admitted record for a canonical ID. An unknown,\n463|// malformed, or never-admitted ID is CodeNotFound: a registry read failure is\n464|// never absence, and callers must not fall back to a default.\n465|func (registry *Registry) Resolve(backendID string) (Registration, error) {\n466|\tif registry == nil {\n467|\t\treturn Registration{}, &Error{Code: CodeNotFound, Detail: \"registry unavailable\"}\n468|\t}\n469|\tid, err := ParseID(backendID)\n470|\tif err != nil {\n471|\t\treturn Registration{}, err\n472|\t}\n473|\tregistry.mutex.RLock()\n474|\tdefer registry.mutex.RUnlock()\n475|\trecord, known := registry.records[id]\n476|\tif !known {\n477|\t\treturn Registration{}, &Error{Code: CodeNotFound, BackendID: id, Detail: \"unregistered terminal_backend_id\"}\n478|\t}\n479|\t// Hand back a copy: the struct header copy shares the backing arrays,\n480|\t// so returning the stored record would hand out live interior state.\n481|\treturn cloneRecord(record), nil\n482|}\n483|\n484|// IDs returns the sorted admitted identities. It reports membership only,\n485|// never availability.\n486|func (registry *Registry) IDs() []string {\n487|\tif registry == nil {\n488|\t\treturn nil\n489|\t}\n490|\tregistry.mutex.RLock()\n491|\tdefer registry.mutex.RUnlock()\n492|\tids := make([]string, 0, len(registry.records))\n493|\tfor id := range registry.records {\n494|\t\tids = append(ids, id)\n495|\t}\n496|\tsort.Strings(ids)\n497|\treturn ids\n498|}\n499|\n500|// DefaultForPlatform returns the platform default for new activation (§6.5):\n(more lines below)","correlation_facts":{"tool_name":"read_file","outcome":"success"}}}
